<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piltene</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pilten</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Piltene, in German Pilten, is a small town on the Venta river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Kurzeme</a>, western Latvia. Its castle was a residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Courland. After the bishopric was sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1559, Magnus of Holstein ruled from it and died there in 1583, and the surrounding District of Pilten remained a separate territory under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a> until 1795.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piltene.jpg" alt="Piltene" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: original download by Kikos, transferred to the Commons by Furfur · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piltenes_pilsdrupas_un_bazn%C4%ABca.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
